 "name": "Corridor Lighting: Brightness and Uniformity of Ten Variants",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Pracki & Krupiński (2021), \"Brightness and Uniformity Perception of Virtual Corridor with Artificial Lighting Systems\", Energies 14:412. Ten computer visualisations (Autodesk 3ds Max, luminance checked against DIALux) of the same empty corridor lit by six luminaire types in ten layouts are shown one after another in the paper's order. For each variant participants assess the brightness and the uniformity of the ceiling, the wall, the floor and the entire corridor on 7-point semantic differential scales (Extremely bright … Extremely dim; Extremely uniform … Extremely non-uniform), coded 7 = extremely bright/uniform to 1 = extremely dim/non-uniform as in the paper. Analysis as in the paper: mean, median, min, max and SD per variant and plane, and the Wald–Wolfowitz runs test to compare groups or viewing conditions. Visualisations are cropped from Figures 4–13 of the open-access paper (CC BY 4.0) with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) online on the participant's own screen; the original compared a controlled box (wall luminance about 10 cd/m²) with an uncontrolled computer room; (2) the paper lists the personal-data topics (gender, age, education, occupation, sight, well-being) but not their answer options, so the options here are written for this template; (3) the paper questionnaire is shown on screen next to each image instead of on paper; (4) images are about 1,800 px wide from the published figures.",
